    "content": "There have been seven documented ties in Oscar history, with the most recent occurring at the 98th Academy Awards in 2026, when \"The Singers\" and \"Two People Exchanging Saliva\" both won for Best Live Action Short. These rare occurrences span acting, documentary, sound, and short film categories, with a famous, non-true tie occurring in 1932. Major Oscar Ties in History 2026 (98th Academy Awards): Best Live Action Short Film tied between The Singers and Two People Exchanging Saliva. 2013 (85th Academy Awards): Best Sound Editing tied between Skyfall (Per Hallberg and Karen Baker Landers) and Zero Dark Thirty (Paul N.J. Ottosson). 1995 (67th Academy Awards): Best Live Action Short Film tied between Franz Kafka’s It’s a Wonderful Life and Trevor. 1987 (59th Academy Awards): Best Documentary Feature tied between Artie Shaw: Time Is All You’ve Got and Down and Out in America. 1969 (41st Academy Awards): Best Actress tied between Katharine Hepburn (The Lion in Winter) and Barbra Streisand (Funny Girl). 1950 (22nd Academy Awards): Best Documentary (Short Subject) tied between A Chance to Live and So Much for So Little. 1932 (5th Academy Awards): Best Actor tied between Fredric March (Dr. Jekyll and Mr. Hyde) and Wallace Beery (The Champ). Technically, March had one more vote, but rules at the time mandated a tie if nominees were within three votes of each other. Notable Context Ties are extremely rare, with only seven instances in nearly 100 years of Academy Awards history. The 2013 Sound Editing tie prompted a surprise announcement by presenter Mark Wahlberg, who assured the audience it was \"No B.S.!\". The 2026 tie was the second time in history the Best Live Action Short category resulted in a tie.",
